@flexiarg baldwin/stationarity-decides-what-can-be-fixed
@title Environmental Stationarity Decides What Should Be Fixed
@sigils [👓/生]
@keywords environment, fixed-target, task-distribution, plasticity, generalization, p0
@audience experiment designers, MetaCA researchers
@tone conditional
@style pattern
@childof baldwin/mechanism
@references [baldwin/two-claims-not-one baldwin/test-evolution-of-learnability-before-static-assimilation]
@references-extra ["Scheiner et al. (2017), Genetic assimilation, the Baldwin effect, and evolutionary rescue, Ecology and Evolution 7:8788-8803" "Aguilar, Bennati and Helbing (2019), How learning can change the course of evolution, PLOS ONE 14:e0219502"]

! conclusion:
  Fix only structure that is invariant over the environment being selected. A single
  stable target can favor genetic assimilation; a predictable task distribution can
  favor inherited learning biases; a rapidly moving or poorly predictable target can
  rationally preserve plasticity.

  + context:
    Hinton and Nowlan use one fixed twenty-bit target. MetaCA evaluations can vary the
    initial phenotype, damage site, and random seed. Fixing `p0` removes one source of
    variation, but it changes the scientific question from robust adaptation over a
    distribution to assimilation of one instance.

  + IF:
    Plasticity responds to environmental or evaluation-specific variation.

  + HOWEVER:
    Calling variable environments "noise" may erase the very reason plasticity is
    adaptive. Conversely, changing the target independently for every evaluation may
    make a static target impossible and render an assimilation test structurally unfair.
    And stationarity has more than one axis: fixing the environment does not make the
    target stationary if the adaptation operator is itself stochastic across lifetimes.

  + THEN:
    Declare the target level before running: one fixed environment for Hinton--Nowlan
    replication, a training distribution for evolution of learnability, and a disjoint
    held-out distribution for generalization. Measure which features remain invariant
    across that distribution and permit assimilation claims only about those features.
    Enumerate every source of cross-lifetime variation and say which the design holds
    fixed, rather than fixing the most visible one and inferring stationarity.

    + CHECK:
      Measure it rather than declaring it: for a fixed genome, how reproducible is the
      adapted state across evaluations? Against chance, and against the inherited
      starting point. A design that cannot answer this has not established that its
      learning has a target to assimilate.

  + BECAUSE:
    The value of plasticity is conditional on environmental predictability. Stable
    regularities reward inherited specialization; residual variation rewards adaptive
    response. Selection should divide labour between genotype and lifetime adaptation
    rather than drive every degree of freedom to one side.

    + EVIDENCE:
      MetaCA now has the measurement rather than the assumption. Per-cell agreement
      between live and frozen reads, taken with the genotype held fixed and only `p0`
      varying, is unimodal (mean `0.290`, sd `0.089`) with zero cells above `0.6`,
      spatially coherent within a run (lag-1 `+0.422`) and uncorrelated across seeds
      (`-0.010`, `-0.060`, `+0.111`). The adaptive answer is structured but indexed by
      environment, so a per-cell genome cannot carry it.

      The fixed-`p0` arms then failed to lower dependence (`0.9844` and `0.9609`, held
      fractions `0.0156` and `0.0391` against a mutation-only expectation of `0.1858`),
      and the second stationarity axis is the candidate reason: the rewrite draws a
      random source bit per cell per step from a stream seeded per evaluation, so two
      lifetimes of the same genome on the same fixed `p0` still diverge. Fixing the
      environment was necessary and not sufficient.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If fixed `p0` enables static replacement but held-out performance collapses, the
      run demonstrates instance-specific canalization, not general MetaCA assimilation.
      If holding both the environment and the operator tape fixed still yields an
      unreproducible adapted state, there is no target at any level of stationarity.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Keep fixed-p0 and distributional-task experiments as different registered claims.
    - Include held-out environments whenever the claim concerns evolved learnability.
    - Enumerate stationarity axes in the registration; environment and operator randomness are separate treatments.
